---
// The five lesson blocks. They are §1–§5 of public/js/cbf.js, in the same order.
// Everything inside .x is what Expert mode reveals, in place.
// Greek letters are wrapped in .up: an italic α is hard to tell from an italic a.
---
<section class="lesson">
  <h2>How it works</h2>

  <!-- 1 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>1</i>Where the end effector is</h3>
    <span class="eq">p(q) = <span class="up">Σ</span><sub>i</sub> L<sub>i</sub> ( cos(q₁ + … + q<sub>i</sub>) ,  sin(q₁ + … + q<sub>i</sub>) )<a class="ref" href="#r1">[1]</a></span>
    <p>Each angle is measured against the link before it, so the angles simply add up.
    Everything below is, by default, a rule about that one point at the end — Expert mode extends
    it to the whole arm — and the formula does not care whether there are two joints or four.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <span class="eq">ṗ = J(q) q̇ ,<span class="c">column i of J is</span>ẑ × ( p − joint i )<a class="ref" href="#r1">[1]</a></span>
      <p>Spinning one joint sweeps everything beyond it around that joint, and moves nothing
      before it. That single sentence is the whole Jacobian, for any number of joints, and it
      is why <em>every</em> point on the arm has one of its own — which is what lets the filter
      protect a link and not only the tip. With two joints J is square and det J = L₁L₂ sin q₂
      vanishes when the arm is straight or folded, and the radial direction is then unreachable
      at any joint speed. With three or four the arm is redundant: J is wide, whole families of
      joint motions produce the same tip motion. The damped inverse below does not pick among them
      exactly: for λ &gt; 0 it minimises ‖J u − v‖² + λ²‖u‖², trading tracking error against joint
      speed, and only tends to the minimum-norm solution as λ → 0.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 2 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>2</i>Where you want to go</h3>
    <span class="eq">v = (p* − p) · <span class="up">min</span>( k<sub>p</sub> ,
      <span class="frac"><span>v<sub>max</sub></span><span>‖p* − p‖</span></span> )
      <span class="c">then</span> u<sub>nom</sub> = J<sup>T</sup> ( J J<sup>T</sup> + <span class="up">λ</span>²I )<sup>−1</sup> v<a class="ref" href="#r2">[2]</a><a class="ref" href="#r3">[3]</a></span>
    <p>Head straight at the target, never faster than the speed cap. This controller has never
    heard of the obstacle and it is never replanned — the detours you are about to see are the
    filter’s doing, not a smarter plan.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <p>The cap is applied to the Cartesian velocity <em>before</em> the inverse, so the
      unfiltered command is a straight line at constant speed until the end effector is within
      v<sub>max</sub>/k<sub>p</sub> of the target, after which it decays proportionally — the
      reference the detour is read against. The realised path is close to that line but not
      exactly on it, because the damped inverse trades tracking accuracy for bounded joint speed. The inverse is damped least squares: J J<sup>T</sup> is 2×2 however many
      joints there are, and its determinant is at least λ⁴ &gt; 0 for
      every pose, so there is no singular case to special-case. Writing
      <code>if (det &lt; ε)</code> would put back exactly the discontinuity that damping exists
      to remove. λ is a length and scales with the arm (0.10 m here);
      it caps the joint-speed gain, so near full stretch the arm refuses the radial command and
      sweeps sideways instead of exploding. Anything genuinely time-optimal would need a
      double-integrator model and a higher-order CBF — a different lesson.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 3 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>3</i>What is forbidden</h3>
    <span class="eq">h(p) = √(ab) · [ ( <span class="frac"><span>|x′|</span><span>a</span></span><sup>n</sup>
      + <span class="frac"><span>|y′|</span><span>b</span></span><sup>n</sup> )<sup>1/n</sup> − 1 ]
      <span class="c">with</span> n = <span class="frac"><span>2</span><span><span class="up">ε</span></span></span><a class="ref" href="#r4">[4]</a><a class="ref" href="#r5">[5]</a></span>
    <table class="h">
      <tr><td>h &gt; 0</td><td>outside — safe</td></tr>
      <tr><td>h = 0</td><td>exactly on the boundary</td></tr>
      <tr><td>h &lt; 0</td><td>inside — the rule is violated</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>One formula draws every forbidden region: ε = 1 is the ellipse
    you drag out yourself, and a small ε squares off the corners so the
    region hugs the rectangular obstacle. The obstacle and a region you drew are the same rule,
    one more h. The √(ab) in front turns the shape value into metres of clearance, so h is very
    nearly the gap you can see on screen.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <span class="eq"><span class="frac"><span>∂h</span><span>∂x</span></span> = √(ab) ·
        ( <span class="frac"><span>|x′| / a</span><span>F</span></span> )<sup>n−1</sup> ·
        <span class="up">sgn</span>(x′) / a <span class="c">F is the bracket above</span></span>
      <p>Taking the n-th root of the superquadric sum is a monotone rescale that fixes zero, so
      the zero level set, the safe set and the CBF property are all untouched — but the raw sum
      grows like distance<sup>n</sup>. At ε = 0.25 that is the eighth power, so an unrooted h would
      reach about 10<sup>6</sup> at the edge of the workspace and throttle the arm to a fraction of
      a sensible approach speed while it is still half a metre away. With the root, ‖∇h‖ stays bounded: it never exceeds √(a/b), and for an ellipse (ε = 1) it never
      falls below √(b/a). For smaller ε it dips a little further — 0.71 rather than 0.78 on the
      default obstacle. h is therefore proportional to the true clearance up to a bounded factor,
      and for a circle it is exactly the signed distance, which is what lets one α mean much the
      same thing for every shape. The absolute values cause no kink for ε &lt; 2. ∇h fails to exist at exactly one point, the
      centre, where the rescaled norm has a conical cusp; the code returns an outward direction
      there rather than dividing by zero. There is no separate
      clearance term: the outline you see <em>is</em> the h = 0 curve.</p>
      <p>The obstacle is a rectangle and stays one; ε shapes the region
      <em>around</em> it, which is always the superellipse that just contains the rectangle — at
      the corner (a, b) we need (a/A)<sup>n</sup> + (b/B)<sup>n</sup> = 1, so
      A = 2<sup>1/n</sup>·a. At ε → 0 the region is the rectangle
      itself; at ε = 1 it is the ellipse circumscribing it.</p>
      <p>On vocabulary: the set where h ≥ 0 is the <em>safe set</em>, and that name is standard.
      Its complement has no single canonical name — papers say unsafe set or avoid set,
      applications say obstacle, and aerospace work says keep-out zone. “Forbidden region” is the
      phrasing used here, and it is also the established term in the virtual-fixtures literature,
      where a forbidden-region virtual fixture<a class="ref" href="#r6">[6]</a> is exactly this: a software constraint that
      keeps a tool out of a volume.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>4</i>How h is allowed to change</h3>
    <span class="eq">ḣ = <span class="frac"><span>∂h</span><span>∂p</span></span>
      <span class="frac"><span>∂p</span><span>∂q</span></span> q̇
      = <span class="up">∇</span>h(p)<sup>T</sup> J(q) u  ≥  − <span class="up">α</span>(h)
      <span class="c">with</span> <span class="up">α</span>(h) = <span class="up">α</span> h<a class="ref" href="#r7">[7]</a><a class="ref" href="#r8">[8]</a></span>
    <p>The rule is not “never get closer than a metre”. It is: <em>the closer you are, the slower
    you may approach.</em> −α(h) is an approach budget that uses itself
    up — on the boundary h = 0, so the budget is zero, so ḣ ≥ 0, and you are guided
    <em>along</em> the boundary instead of through it. Because h is in metres,
    α reads off directly: at α = 2 you may
    close at 2 m/s for every metre of clearance, so at 10 cm you may close at 20 cm/s. α has units
    of 1/s.</p>
    <p><strong>The plot under the picture is this line.</strong> The curve is −α(h),
    the fastest the clearance is allowed to fall; the dot is the arm right now. Everything above
    the curve is permitted, and while the safety filter is on the dot cannot drop below it. Watch it slide down into the
    corner as the arm arrives: at h = 0 the only permitted rate is zero.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <span class="eq"><span class="up">α</span>(h) = <span class="up">α</span> · <span class="up">sgn</span>(h) · |h|<sup>p</sup> ,
        <span class="c">and for p = 1</span> h(t) ≥ h(0) e<sup>−<span class="up">α</span>t</sup><a class="ref" href="#r9">[9]</a></span>
      <p>α·h is the simplest extended class-K function: through the origin, strictly increasing,
      and defined for h &lt; 0 as well, which is what makes the inequality meaningful inside a
      region. With that choice the comparison lemma gives the bound above — forward invariance from
      h(0) ≥ 0, and exponential recovery from h(0) &lt; 0. The general form is extended class-K for
      every p &gt; 0, and the sign factor is what keeps it increasing through zero: α·h<sup>p</sup>
      alone would be positive on both sides for even p. p = 1 is the textbook straight line and
      p = 2 is the paper’s h²; larger p buys caution close to the boundary and freedom far from it,
      and turns the recovery bound from exponential into a power law. The slider starts at p = 1
      because for p &lt; 1 the function is no longer locally Lipschitz at the origin and the
      comparison lemma no longer applies; in this simulation p = 0.5 lets the Euler step overshoot the
      boundary by up to 1.7 mm.</p>
      <p>Because the robot is velocity-commanded, u appears already in the <em>first</em> derivative
      of h, so wherever ∇h<sup>T</sup>J ≠ 0 the constraint has relative degree 1 and a single
      inequality linear in u suffices. Where that row vanishes the arm has no authority over h at
      all, and the filter reports it rather than pretending otherwise. Command torque instead and u
      drops out of ḣ entirely, and a higher-order CBF<a class="ref" href="#r10">[10]</a> is needed.</p>
      <p>The guarantee is continuous-time. We integrate with explicit Euler at Δt = 1/240 s, where
      αΔt ≤ 1 keeps the <em>linearised</em> update h<sub>k+1</sub> ≥ (1 − αΔt)h<sub>k</sub>
      non-negative; at the largest α on the slider αΔt ≈ 0.08. That is not a discrete-time proof —
      the neglected second-order term is what could let a trajectory dip past the boundary — but over
      the parameter sweeps run for this page no violation occurred for p ≥ 1, though at the tightest
      setting the remaining margin was under a micrometre.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5 ------------------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <div class="step">
    <h3><i>5</i>The filter</h3>
    <span class="eq">u = <span class="up">arg min</span> ‖u − u<sub>nom</sub>‖²
      <span class="c">subject to</span> <span class="up">∇</span>h<sup>T</sup> J u ≥ − <span class="up">α</span>(h)
      <span class="c">for every guarded point</span><a class="ref" href="#r7">[7]</a></span>
    <p>If the command is already safe, the filter hands it back untouched — that is what it means
    when the ghost arrow is nowhere to be seen, sitting exactly under the solid one. Otherwise it
    makes the smallest change that obeys every rule at once.</p>
    <div class="x">
      <h4>Standard: guarding the end effector</h4>
      <p>The objective is strictly convex, so the solution is the unique Euclidean projection of
      u<sub>nom</sub> onto the feasible polyhedron, and at the optimum some subset of the rows holds
      with equality. More rows than that can be tight, but the correction u − u<sub>nom</sub> lies in
      the cone they generate, and in ℝ<sup>n</sup> such a cone is generated by at most n of them, so
      subsets of size n suffice. Enumerating the subsets, solving the
      equality-constrained problem for each, and retaining the feasible candidate of least cost
      therefore returns the exact optimum, and requires no multiplier sign test. Most rows are
      eliminated before the enumeration begins: on the safe set u = 0 is feasible, so the optimum
      satisfies ‖u − u<sub>nom</sub>‖ ≤ ‖u<sub>nom</sub>‖, and any row whose slack exceeds
      ‖a‖·‖u<sub>nom</sub>‖ cannot be active.</p>
      <p>Feasibility is guaranteed on the safe set: if every h ≥ 0 then every right-hand side
      −α(h) ≤ 0, so u = 0 satisfies every row. Infeasibility therefore requires at least one
      h &lt; 0, and the enumeration certifies it at no additional cost. The arm is then stopped and
      the condition reported. Stopping does not restore safety — with h &lt; 0 the row demands ḣ &gt; 0
      and u = 0 delivers ḣ = 0 — but it fails visibly, whereas a slack variable would report success
      on a constraint it had just relaxed.</p>

      <h4>Link guarding: the closest point of each link</h4>
      <span class="eq">p<sub>k</sub>(s) = (1 − s) j<sub>k−1</sub> + s j<sub>k</sub> ,
        <span class="c">and</span> s*<sub>k</sub> = <span class="up">arg min</span><sub>s∈[0,1]</sub> h( p<sub>k</sub>(s) )</span>
      <span class="eq">a<sub>k</sub> = J(q, k, s*<sub>k</sub>)<sup>T</sup> <span class="up">∇</span>h( p<sub>k</sub>(s*<sub>k</sub>) ) ,
        <span class="c">and</span> b<sub>k</sub> = − <span class="up">α</span>(h)<a class="ref" href="#r11">[11]</a></span>
      <p>Instead of a single row for the end effector, one row per link, written at that link’s
      closest point to the region. h is convex along a straight line, so a ternary search locates
      the minimiser to about 10<sup>−5</sup> of the link length in 28 iterations. Where that minimiser
      is unique, Danskin’s theorem<a class="ref" href="#r12">[12]</a> gives the derivative of the
      minimum as the derivative at the minimiser, so the row may be evaluated at the frozen
      parameter s*<sub>k</sub>; a region with a nearly flat face can make it non-unique, and there
      the minimum is only directionally differentiable and the row takes one element of the
      subdifferential. This is both tighter and cheaper than
      sampling points along the link. With three or four joints and the option disabled, a forearm
      passes through the region while the end effector goes around it.</p>

      <h4>Self-collision guarding: the manipulator against itself</h4>
      <span class="eq">d<sub>ij</sub> = <span class="up">min</span><sub>s,t</sub> ‖ p<sub>i</sub>(s) − p<sub>j</sub>(t) ‖ ,
        <span class="c">and</span> n̂ = <span class="frac"><span>p<sub>j</sub>(t*) − p<sub>i</sub>(s*)</span><span>d<sub>ij</sub></span></span></span>
      <span class="eq">ḋ<sub>ij</sub> = n̂<sup>T</sup> ( J<sub>j</sub>(t*) − J<sub>i</sub>(s*) ) u ≥
        − <span class="up">α</span>( d<sub>ij</sub> − d<sub>min</sub> )<a class="ref" href="#r11">[11]</a></span>
      <p>The same construction with the manipulator as both bodies: h is the clearance between two
      non-adjacent links — their distance less the margin d<sub>min</sub>, as the second formula
      shows — s* and t* are the parameters of their closest points, and the row is the
      difference of the two link Jacobians projected onto the line joining those points. Adjacent
      links share a joint, so their distance is identically zero and no such constraint can be
      imposed; the option therefore has no effect below three joints.</p>

      <h4>What the filter does not promise</h4>
      <p>The objective is minimal in <em>joint</em> space, not in task space: the task-space
      correction direction is J J<sup>T</sup>∇h rather than ∇h, so the end effector also
      decelerates as it slides along a boundary rather than only changing direction. This is a
      property of the formulation, not an artefact. Safety is likewise not liveness — the arm may
      stall with the target behind a region, and nothing in the construction precludes it. A safety
      filter certifies constraint satisfaction; reaching the goal remains the planner’s task.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <blockquote>
    <b>WHAT is forbidden lives in h. HOW CAUTIOUSLY you approach lives in α.</b>
    <span>Draw a new shape and you have changed h. Move one slider and you have changed α.
    Nothing else in the controller needs to know.</span>
  </blockquote>
</section>

